Currently the situation at the type-level is following:

```typescript
export interface ZodInt extends ZodNumberFormat {}
export interface ZodFloat32 extends ZodNumberFormat {}
export interface ZodFloat64 extends ZodNumberFormat {}
export interface ZodInt32 extends ZodNumberFormat {}
export interface ZodUInt32 extends ZodNumberFormat {}
export declare function int(...): ZodInt;
export declare function float32(...): ZodFloat32;
export declare function float64(...): ZodFloat64;
export declare function int32(...): ZodInt32;
export declare function uint32(...): ZodUInt32;
```

Also same with ZodBigIntFormat, they do have string-literal types but no ability to narrow, since both return same type:

```typescript
export declare function int64(...): ZodBigIntFormat;
export declare function uint64(...): ZodBigIntFormat;
```

This makes it impossible to use these to for instance derive Postgres types which are required to exist typed at type-level too (for Kysely etc).

## Suggestion

What I would like them to be is something like:

```typescript
export interface ZodInt extends ZodNumberFormat {
  _zod: {
    def: {
      format: "safeint"
    }
  }
}
export interface ZodFloat32 extends ZodNumberFormat {
  _zod: {
    def: {
      format: "float32"
    }
  }
}
export interface ZodFloat64 extends ZodNumberFormat {
  _zod: {
    def: {
      format: "float64"
    }
  }
}
export interface ZodInt32 extends ZodNumberFormat {
  _zod: {
    def: {
      format: "int32"
    }
  }
}
export interface ZodUInt32 extends ZodNumberFormat {
  _zod: {
    def: {
      format: "uint32"
    }
  }
}
```

This would allow me to use that format (which already exists in runtime) as differentiator.

Thanks.

When defining a Record with enums as keys and values with defaults, then zod is able to complete the missing keys with the default values in the parsed result:
```ts
import z from "zod"

const schema = z.record(z.enum(["UP", "DOWN"]), z.string().default("unknown"))

type Input = z.input<typeof schema>

// @ts-ignore
const input: Input = {
    UP: "somewhere"
}

console.log(z.parse(schema, input))
```
output:
```
{ UP: 'somewhere', DOWN: 'unknown' }
```

The only issue is that the inferred type `Input` is:
```ts
type Input = {
    UP: string | undefined;
    DOWN: string | undefined;
}
```
which requires using `// @ts-ignore` when defining partial inputs, otherwise typescript complains with:
```
error TS2741: Property 'DOWN' is missing in type '{ UP: string; }' but required in type 'Record<"UP" | "DOWN", string | undefined>
```

Assuming it is intended for zod to generate the missing fields in the output, then it would be amazing if the inferred `z.input` type to be Partial. For example:
```ts
type Input = Partial<{
    UP: string | undefined;
    DOWN: string | undefined;
}>
```

